Porous materials encompass a wide range of inorganic, hybrid organic–inorganic and organic porous materials. Whilst porous inorganic materials such as metal oxides and zeolites have been established for some time, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), porous organic polymers (POPs) and covalentorganic frameworks (COFs) have been discovered more recently. Over the past two decades, the development of these novel porous materials, $\mathbf{MOFS}^{5-8}$ and COFs,9–13 porous organic cages,14–16 porous organic polymers17,18 and metal–organic/dynamic covalent gels,19,20 has experienced tremendous progress. These extended molecular porous materials are typically constructed from molecular building blocks that are linked together by dynamic covalent or coordinative bonds. The resulting materials are often highly porous, contain remarkably high surface areas and can be easily chemically functionalized.
MOFs, in particular, have been extensively studied owing to their porous nature, large surface area and interesting host and guest properties, leading to a wide range of potential applications.21 Their hybrid structures, combinations of inorganic and organic moieties, give a wide range of complex architectures with resulting properties that can be applied to numerous important fields, including porosity for molecular storage, sieving, separation and sensing,22,23 heterogeneous catalysis,24,25 drug delivery,26 and energy storage.27 The stability of MOFs is, however, still a key issue that constrains many applications and further commercialisation.
Some of the most common MOF materials including MIL-101,28 MIL-53,29–31 UiO-66,32–34 MOF-5,35,36 HKUST- $\cdot1^{37}$ and ZIF- $\cdot8^{38,39}$ have been extensively studied (see the $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ accompanying this review for a brief introduction to these MOF materials) (Fig. 1).
POPs are a class of highly crosslinked, amorphous polymers possessing nanopores, and have attracted increasing research interest because of the combined advantage of porous materials and functional polymers. POPs exhibit well-defined porosity, high surface area, and tuneable surface chemistry; in addition, they are lightweight and can be easily functionalized. In order to maintain inner cavities, highly rigid polymer networks must be constructed to prevent the collapse of polymer chains into a nonporous dense state, and the key strategy for this purpose is the use of rigid building units fixed with strong covalent bonds. POPs can be developed for various applications, such as in gas adsorption and separation, supercapacitors, optoelectronic devices,40,41 chemosensors, catalysis and biomedical applications.42 As an emerging material, POPs offer high flexibility for the
