Covalent-organic frameworks (COFs) are attractive materials for their fascinating properties, such as their rigid structure, exceptional thermal stability, low density, and permanent porosity with specific surface areas. COFs are a well-established class of porous and crystalline materials in which the organic building blocks are firmly held together by covalent bonds, with atomically precise design, highly ordered organic building blocks and discrete pores, which is in contrast to POPs. Composed of light elements (C, H, B, N and O),44 the unique features of periodic and tuneable porous structures, large surface area, low density and abundant functional groups on the channel walls make COFs excellent candidates for a variety of applications in gas adsorption, separation,45 catalysis,46,47 optoelectronic devices and sensing.48 COF- $5^{49}$ and COF-LZU150 are two representative two-dimensional COF materials; COF-5 is boronate ester linked, and COF-LZU1 is imine linked (Scheme 1) (see the ESI† accompanying this review for a brief introduction to these COF materials).
Porous organic cage solids are a class of porous materials assembled from discrete, covalently bonded porous molecules that possess intrinsic, shape-persistent voids. Several families of porous organic cages are based on dynamic covalent chemistry. Discrete molecules are held together by weak intermolecular forces, so structural isomerism is prevalent for porous molecular solids and different amorphous or crystalline solid-state phases may be formed for a single molecular cage. The soluble nature of porous organic cages offers solution-processing options. Porous organic cage solids have found applications in separation, sensing and catalysis. For example, Cooper and co-workers have discovered a range of imine-based cages with high permanent porosity, which are formed by the condensation of 1,3,5-triformylbenzene or tris(4-formylphenyl)amine with various diamines (Fig. 2).51,52 Among these cages, CC3-R is assembled by the $\left[4\substack{+}6\right]$ cycloimination reaction of 1,3,5-triformylbenzene and $^{(R,R)}$ -1,2-diaminocyclohexane, while the opposite cage enantiomer, CC3-S, is formed from $^{S,S-1,2}$ -cyclohexanediamine. CC3-R has a tetrahedral symmetry and includes four windows that can be penetrated by small molecules. In the solid state, CC3-R packs window-towindow, resulting in a 3D interconnected pore structure that runs through the centre of each cage, which leads to high levels of permanent microporosity in the crystals after desolvation, with apparent BET surface areas of up to 800 m2 g1.53,54 CC9 and
CC10 are assembled from the cycloimination of 1,3,5-triformylbenzene with $^{(R,R)}$ -1,2-diphenylethylenediamine and $^{(R,R)-1,2-}$ bis(4-fluorophenyl)ethane-1,2-diamine, respectively. CC9 and CC10 with bulky aryl groups attached to the vertices show enhanced porosity by creating additional, extrinsic porosity. The materials exhibit BET surface areas of up to $\sim850~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .55
In this review, we discuss emerging porous materials and their many applications and uses as functional materials within capillary columns and microfluidic devices for separation, catalysis and others (Fig. 3). This article is structured into sections that cover porous materials and their use in capillary columns; applications of porous materials in chromatography; porous materials in microfluidic catalysis and membrane fabrication, and incorporation of porous materials in microfluidic devices. The packing of porous materials, the development of monoliths in columns and the coating of porous materials on the capillary surface walls are reviewed as three major strategies for achieving separation and catalysis with porous materials in flow systems (Section 2). For the applications of porous materials in microchannels, chromatographic separations in capillary columns (Section 3), continuous flow catalytic synthesis (Section 4) and membranes of porous materials on hollow supports (Section 5) are summarized. The progress made in the incorporation of porous materials into microfluidic devices has been highlighted for various functions (Section 6). Finally challenges and future opportunities in the field are highlighted.
## 2. Porous materials and columns
Emerging porous materials are expanding the applications of microfluidics, while at the same time microfluidic systems provide robust and flexible platforms for the fabrication of porous materials.56–58 Even before the introduction of the concept of microfluidics, microchannels had been used in the form of glass capillaries in chromatography, and porous materials have emerged as stationary phases for chromatographic separations.59
The application of porous materials in chromatography, for example, relies on combining the porous material with appropriately enclosed channels, capillaries or columns. Commercially available fused-silica and stainless steel are commonly used, whilst other materials such as silicon, glass, ceramics or polymers can also be used to form enclosed microchannel structures.60 Fused-silica capillaries are particularly versatile since they have reactive silanol groups (Si–OH) on their surface that can react with silanes which enable the attachment of various types of functionalised porous materials, while stainless steel columns are often used for packed columns.61 Capillary columns can be broadly classified into three types: packed capillary columns, monolithic capillary columns and open tubular capillary columns, as shown in Fig. 4.62
## 2.1 Packed columns
Solid porous materials can be directly packed in capillary columns in the form of fine powder particles. Such packed columns are often fast, straightforward and convenient to achieve. The packed column is simply a hollow tube, pipe, or other vessels that are filled with a packing material and can be used for chromatography or as a reactor (Fig. 5).63,64 Porous materials can be loaded into the columns by manual filling or by applying a pump or vacuum at the outlet. The materials are retained inside the columns by constrictions at the ends with filters or micropillars. For example, Dai and co-workers packed single crystals of MOF-508 in a chromatographic column for GC separation of alkanes based on their different van der Waals interactions arising from subtle size- and shape-selective matching.63 A porous polymer monolith or a stainless steel frit with pores is usually used as a frit to retain particulate packings in the capillary. Packed columns can be assembled in chromatography or in packed-bed reactors for flow synthesis. Their key advantage is the facile incorporation of porous materials; however, drawbacks include random packing of materials beads, heat transfer limitation, uncontrolled fluid dynamics, unwanted wall flow inside the columns, and significant pressure drops due to the accommodation of fine particles.
To overcome the problem of high back pressure caused by directly using nanosized porous materials as the stationary phase, the combination of silica spheres with a few $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ size and nanosized materials has been used as the stationary phase. A UiO-66-silica composite has been fabricated by a hydrothermal method and then applied as a liquid chromatographic stationary phase for fast and efficient separation (Fig. 6a).65 Fast separation of ethylbenzene and styrene is achieved on the silica-UiO-66 composite packed column within $1.6\mathrm{{min}}$ .
## 2.2 Monolithic columns
Monoliths have a unibody structure composed of interconnected channels. Silica or polymer monoliths usually used feature a hierarchical porosity of macro- and mesopores.64,67,68 Composite monoliths of porous materials are in situ prepared by mixing with organic polymers or inorganic silica (Fig. 5). For chromatographic and catalytic application, porous monolithic materials possessing pore sizes in the range of macro- and mesopores are preferred. The mesopores provide adequate surface required for solute retention, and the interconnected large macropores allow the mobile phase to flow through the stationary phase and are beneficial for rapid mass transfer properties leading to high flow rates and low column back pressure. A monolithic column characterized by $2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ macropores and $13\ \mathrm{nm}$ mesopores has an equivalent column efficiency to porous silica particles of $3{-}3.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ .69 Monoliths can offer certain advantages including robustness, easy handling, lower flow resistance, and essential supports for catalysis and separation applications. For example, Huang and co-workers reported in situ monolithic polymer syntheses on a fused silica capillary for capillary electrochromatography and nanoscale liquid chromatography/mass spectrometry.70 Prior to the monolith syntheses, vinyl groups are covalently attached onto the capillary inner wall ( $100~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ I.D.) to avoid total or partial detachment of the monolithic materials from the capillary. The monolithic materials demonstrate high reproducibility and promising performances when they were employed as separation columns in chromatography. Monolith columns have several advantages such as high void volume, large surface area, little resistance to flow, a low pressure drop, and efficient mass transfer through their hierarchical porosity. Monolithic supports show fast mass transport between the monolithic support (stationary phase, catalyst bed) and the surrounding liquid (mobile phase, reaction mixture) in chromatography or in monolithic flow reactors (heterogeneous catalysis).71 Pore clogging is the main problem associated with monolith columns, and wall flow inside the columns may be caused by monolith shrinkage.
## 2.3 Coated-wall columns or open tubular columns
A coated-wall column is a capillary or a channel with a coated inner surface (Fig. 7). Porous materials have been applied to open tubular capillary columns, in which a layer of porous materials are bonded covalently or by other interactions to the inner wall of a fused-silica capillary.72 Mass transfer resistance is substantially minimized for coated-wall columns. There is no pressure drop or blockage of microchannels, while flow dynamics, and heat and mass transfer properties are easily predicted. Fewer materials are needed in coated-wall columns than in packed columns. When assembled in catalytic reactors, the porous coating can be catalytically active or serve as supports for catalytically active centre. Chemical treatment of the channel walls and application of porous coatings may remarkably increase the specific surface area. Moreover, porous materials coating provides the possibility of tuning the catalyst loading, and also easy recycling and reuse of the catalyst, as well easy separation from the reaction mixture for ease of work-up. Limitations include relatively low catalyst loading in a catalytic microreactor.
Methods have been developed to coat porous materials onto capillary columns to form coated-wall columns. Porous materials can be coated onto capillary columns by a dynamic coating (Fig. 8) method by flushing slurries of porous materials through capillaries, and are deposited onto columns by evaporating the solvent from suspensions of porous materials. For example, Yan and co-workers showed that a MIL-101 suspension in ethanol is first filled as a plug into the capillary column under gas pressure, and then pushed through the column to leave a wet coating layer on the inner wall of the capillary column. The column is obtained after further conditioning under nitrogen and heat treatment.73 Surface-coating techniques have been developed for open tubular columns via surface functionalization and subsequent crosslinking of porous materials. For example, fused-silica capillary is first modified with amine functional groups on the inner wall, and then a Mn-salen imine porous gel material can be in situ produced and anchored on the inner wall via imine bonding.74 Fabricating open tubular column coated with porous materials provides a novel method to apply porous materials to analytical fields, catalysis and so on.
## 3. Porous materials for chromatography
Chromatography is a separation technique via differential partition of solute molecules between a stationary phase and a mobile phase. Various MOFs, COFs and porous organic cages have been used in chromatographic separation as stationary phases, such as in high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC), gas chromatography (GC), and capillary electrochromatography (CEC).75–78 Chiral porous materials in chromatography are highlighted separately in this section. The reported porous materials were chosen as the stationary phase mainly considering that they have large surface area, tuneable pore surface and interaction with guest molecules, and excellent chemical and thermal stability.79,80 Among porous materials, crystalline MOFs and COFs have more uniform and regular pore channels than amorphous materials, and structure–performance relationship may be well established. Despite the ready formation of packed and coated-wall columns, all crystalline materials only form monolithic columns in the presence of amorphous polymer materials. For practical applications, high chemical stability of porous materials in the liquid phase is preferred for HPLC and CEC, while high thermal stability is preferred for GC.
## 3.1 High-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC)
HPLC is one of the most widely used analytical separation techniques, in which analytes are separated on the basis of their affinity for a stationary surface. Normal-phase (NP) and reverse-phase (RP)-HPLC have been developed for HPLC. In NP-HPLC, the stationary phase is more polar than the mobile phase, while the opposite for RP-HPLC. Separation using liquid chromatography is usually performed at ambient temperature. Packed columns with pure MOF materials or $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ composites of MOFs and COFs, and monolith columns of MOFs and COFs have been developed for HPLC (Table 1).
Other numbers of the MIL series including MIL-101(Cr) and MIL-100(Fe) have also been applied for HPLC. A slurry-packed MIL-101(Cr) column was investigated for HPLC separation of substituted aromatics by Yan and co-workers.85 The MIL-101(Cr) suspension in $\mathbf{CH}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ was packed into a stainless steel column under 6000 psi for $10\mathrm{min}$ . The MIL-101(Cr) packed column was conditioned with $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ at a flow rate of $1\ \mathrm{mL}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ before chromatographic experiments. Hexane and $\mathbf{CH}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ were used to prepare the mobile phase. The MIL-101(Cr) packed column offers high-resolution separation of ethylbenzene and xylene, dichlorobenzene and chlorotoluene isomers, and ethylbenzene and styrene. The typical impurities of toluene and $o$ -xylene in ethylbenzene and styrene mixtures are also efficiently separated on the column. Additionally, the high affinity for the ortho-isomer allows fast and selective separation of the orthoisomer from the other isomers within 3 min using $\mathbf{CH}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ as the mobile phase. MIL-100(Fe) was explored as a novel stationary phase for both normal-phase and reverse-phase HPLC.86 The mesoporous cages, accessible windows, excellent chemical and solvent stability, metal active sites and aromatic pore walls make MIL-100(Fe) a good candidate as a novel stationary phase for both NP-HPLC and RP-HPLC. The MIL-100(Fe) suspension dispersed in $\mathbf{CH}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ was then downward packed into a stainless steel column under 6000 psi for $10\mathrm{min}$ . The stationary phase was kept intact by the stainless steel frits with a pore size of $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . The packed column was conditioned with MeOH for RP-HPLC experiments or with $\mathbf{CH}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ for NP-HPLC experiments. MIL-100(Fe) shows good selectivity and reproducibility for both RP-HPLC separation of neutral and basic analytes, and NP-HPLC separation of chloroaniline and toluidine isomers due to the presence of active Fe sites.
UiO- $66\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ and UiO-67 were used as the stationary phase in NP-HPLC for separation of substituted benzenes (SBs) and polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs).87 SBs (ethylbenzene, styrene and $o$ -xylene) are well separated on the $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ column but not on the UiO-67 column. Nonetheless, molecules with relatively large diameter PAHs are well separated on the UiO-67 column. The functional groups and pore size of MOFs play important roles in the separation.
ZIF-8 was used as a sorbent for on-line solid-phase extraction coupled with HPLC for the determination of oxytetracycline, tetracycline and chlorotetracycline in water and milk samples by Yan and co-workers.88 ZIF-8 was packed into a stainless steel column which was mounted on the HPLC injector valve to replace the sample loop. On-line solid-phase extraction of oxytetracycline, tetracycline and chlorotetracycline was achieved by loading the sample solution at a flow rate of $3.0\mathrm{mL}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ for $10~\mathrm{min}$ with the aid of a flow-injection system.
A HKUST-1 packed monolith was investigated for HPLC separation by Zhang and co-workers.66 The pure crystalline phase HKUST-1 monolith with a hierarchical pore structure containing micropores, mesopores and macropores is synthesized via powder packing (Fig. 6b). The monolith consists of aggregated particles of around $300\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and showed a surface area of $1240\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ with a micropore volume of $0.561{\mathrm{~cm}}^{3}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ and mesopore volume of $0.274~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Fast separation of $o$ -xylene, $p$ -nitrophenol and thiophene is realized in $1\mathrm{min}$ , and the monolithic column is stable and can be used for at least 6 weeks and over 60 injections for different test mixtures.
3.1.2 Packed columns with $\mathbf{sio}_{2}$ composites of MOFs and COFs. The UiO-66-silica composite packed column previously mentioned65 gives efficient HPLC separation of chlorobenzene compounds, polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs), positional isomers and ethylbenzene and styrene. A diverse range of host– guest interactions, including hydrogen bonding, coordination with metal sites and $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking provided by UiO-66, play important roles in the efficient HPLC separation. HKUST-1 grown on spheres-on-sphere silica particles modified with –COOH and $\mathbf{-NH}_{2}$ groups has also been used for packed columns.90 The packed columns show separation of toluene/ethylbenzene/styrene and toluene/o-xylene/thiophene within $1.5\ \mathrm{min}$ . Monodisperse ZIF- $8@{\mathrm{SiO}}_{2}$ core–shell microspheres were fabricated as the stationary phase for HPLC to overcome the low column efficiency of MOF-packed columns.91 The monodisperse ZIF- ${\mathrm{8@SiO}}_{2}$ core– shell microspheres are prepared through controllable growth of ZIF-8 shells on spherical carboxylate-terminated silica cores. After three cycles of ZIF-8 growth, the diameter of ZIF- $8@{\mathrm{SiO}}_{2}$ core– shell microspheres increases from $3.0~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ for bare $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ microspheres to $3.8~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. 10), and a BET surface area of $565~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a total pore volume of $0.48~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ are achieved. The fabricated monodisperse ZIF- $8@{\mathrm{SiO}}_{2}$ packed columns show high column efficiency (23 000 plates $\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ for bisphenol A) for HPLC separation of endocrine-disrupting chemicals (bisphenol A, $\upbeta$ -estradiol, and $p$ -(tert-octyl)phenol) and pesticides (thiamethoxam, hexaflumuron, chlorantraniliprole, and pymetrozine) within 7 min with good relative standard deviations for 11 replicate separations of the analytes.
While the irregular morphology of COFs is the main obstacle for the practical application of COFs in HPLC, Yan and coworkers reported an in situ growth strategy to synthesize monodispersed ${\mathrm{COF@SiO}}_{2}$ microspheres with uniform and controllable COF shells as the stationary phase for HPLC.92 Spherical $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ is selected as both the core and the source of amino group to react with the aldehyde group on 1,3,5- triformylphloroglucinol $\left(\mathrm{Tp}\right)$ to induce the controllable growth of a TpBD shell on the surface of $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ , and then the resulting $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ -Tp reacts with benzidine (BD) to form the TpBD shell on the silica core (Fig. 11 and Scheme 1). SEM images show that TpBD $\ @\operatorname{SiO}_{2}$ microspheres form regularly, while the thickness of the TpBD shell on $\mathrm{TpBD}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ can be easily controlled by adjusting the concentration of Tp and BD. The $\mathrm{TpBD}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ packed columns show excellent hydrophobic selectivity and good reproducibility for the separation of neutral (toluene and ethylbenzene, polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons), acidic (hydroquinone, $p$ -cresol and $p{\cdot}$ -chlorophenol) and basic molecules (nucleobases, nucleosides and deoxynucleosides).
Silica gel microspheres decorated with a covalent triazinebased framework $\left(\mathrm{CTF}{\cdot}\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\right)$ composite as the stationary phase was reported for HPLC by Zhang and co-workers.93 $\mathbf{CTF-SiO}_{2}$ was prepared through the regular growth of CTF on the surface of spherical cyano-terminated silica $\left(\mathrm{CN-SiO}_{2}\right)$ (Fig. 12). The $\mathbf{CTF-SiO}_{2}$ stationary phase packed column provided rapid and high-resolution separation of several mono-substituted benzenes, polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) and polar compounds with high selectivity. These excellent results are due to the dual benefits of good column packing properties of the silica spheres and the separation ability of the CTF framework. The synergism of triazine and aromatic moieties provided several different retention mechanisms, thus improving the selectivity in the $\mathbf{CTF-SiO}_{2}$ composite.
A hydrazone-linked chiral COF and its $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ composite were fabricated by Zhang and co-workers.94 The BtaMth COF is synthesised from the reaction of 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxaldehyde (Bta) and the chiral building block (S)-2,5-bis(2-methylbutoxy)- terephthalohydrazide (Mth) (Scheme 1), while the BtaMth $\ @\operatorname{SiO}_{2}$ composite was synthesized by employing a similar procedure with the exception that $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}–\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was added. SEM images reveal that the BtaMth COF displays a ball-shaped morphology with a diameter of about $1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , which is composed of nanoscale crystallites. The BtaMth COF is microporous with a BET surface area and total pore volume of ${723}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $0.46~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ respectively. Baseline or at least $80\%$ valley separations of nitrotoluene and nitrochlorobenzene on the BtaMth $@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ column are achieved within $40~\mathrm{min}$ . The elution of these tested aromatic positional isomers followed an increasing order of ortho- $<p a r a–<$ meta-isomers, which is not frequently observed in HPLC separations. In addition, the BtaMth $@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ packed column provided HPLC separation of cis–trans isomers. The chromatograms display baseline separation for $\upbeta\cdot$ -cypermethrin and at least $90\%$ valley separation for metconazole within 60 min. The pore size of the BtaMth COF is significantly larger than the maximum diameters of the above positional isomers, which offers the opportunity for the entrance of analytes into the 1D channels of the BtaMth COF, thus leading to diverse interaction forces rested with different analytes during the HPLC separation processes. The selectivity of BtaMth $\textcircled{2}$ $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ for separations of positional isomers and cis–trans isomers mainly arises from hydrophobic interactions and the diverse lengthto-width ratios of the corresponding isomeric compounds.
3.1.3 Monolithic columns. A methacrylate-bonded COF monolithic column was investigated for HPLC by Yan and co-workers.95 Polymerization of 1,3,5-triformylphloroglucinol (Tp), 1,4-phenylenediamine (Pa), methacrylic anhydride (MA) and ethylene dimethacrylate (EDMA) occurs inside the stainless-steel column to obtain poly(TpPa-MA-co-EDMA) monolithic columns (Fig. 13). The prepared COF bonded monolithic columns not only show good homogeneity and permeability, but also give high column efficiency, good resolution and precision for HPLC separation of small molecules including polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, phenols, anilines, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and benzothiophenes. The good performance relies on the hydrophobic, $\pi{-}\pi$ , and hydrogen bond interactions between analytes and COF TpPa.
## 3.2 Gas chromatography (GC)
For gas chromatography (GC) gaseous analyte is transported through the column by a gaseous mobile phase. A sample is vaporized and injected onto the head of a chromatographic column, and elution is achieved by flowing an inert gaseous mobile phase. Open tubular columns are typically used for GC due to their short analysis time, high resolution, great sensitivity and low sample capacity. The typical diameter of GC capillary columns is $0.10{-}0.53\ \mathrm{mm}$ . GC separation is based on the interaction forces between the solid stationary phase and analyte. High thermal stability is an essential requirement of selected porous materials. Thus porous materials with robust and permanent porosity, high thermal and chemical stability and host–guest interaction sites are usually chosen for coating of GC columns (Table 2).
Various MOF materials can be coated onto pre-treated capillary columns by a dynamic coating method via suspensions. A series of MOF-coated capillary columns have been reported for high-resolution GC separation by Yan and co-workers.73,99–101 MIL-101 was chosen as the stationary phase for high-resolution GC separation of xylene isomers and ethylbenzene (EB),73 while nanosized IRMOFs were chosen to fabricate coated capillary columns for the separation of persistent organic pollutants (POPs), including polychlorinated biphenyls (PCBs), polyaromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs), polybrominated diphenylethers (PBDEs), and hexachlorocyclohexanes (HCHs).99 The IRMOF coated capillary columns have been shown to be promising for the separation of POPs with good reproducibility, high resolution, great separation efficiency, and a wide linear range. UiO-66 was explored for capillary gas chromatographic separation of hexane isomers and benzene homologues with excellent selectivity and precision due to its reverse shape selectivity and molecular sieving effect.100 A UiO-66 coated capillary column gives preferential retention of branched alkane isomers over their linear isomers, showing reverse shape selectivity. Much bulkier 1,3,5-trimethylbenzene is eluted earlier than $n$ -propylbenzene and iso-propylbenzene on the capillary column, exhibiting the molecular sieving effect. The column is also promising for capillary GC separation of dichlorobenzene and chlorotoluene isomers. A ZIF-8 nanocrystal coated capillary shows the ability to sieve branched alkanes from linear alkane isomers due to the molecular sieving effect of narrow pore windows (Fig. 14).101 The capillary also gives high resolution and selectivity for the separation of linear alkanes with a broad range of boiling points and their elution sequence in the order of the carbon numbers. Low boiling point constituents of linear alkanes (hexane, heptane, octane, nonane) are baseline separated without temperature-programmed control, even if the temperature is much higher than their boiling points. The selectivity mainly arises from the van der Waals interaction between linear alkanes and the hydrophobic inner surfaces of the micropores in ZIF-8. The stronger van der Waals interaction between them makes linear alkanes well separated from each other, while the weaker one between branched alkanes and the outer walls of the pores makes the separation of branched alkanes difficult.
While the dynamic coating method for the immobilization of porous materials mainly relies on physical absorption which is unstable for using as a stationary phase, capillary gaschromatographic columns coated with MOF-5102 and HKUST-1103 have been fabricated based on a controlled secondary building unit approach by Mertens and co-workers. The use of self-assembled monolayers enables the generation of a wall-bonded, crosslinked stationary phase for gas chromatographic capillary columns. The MOF-5 column displays excellent performance in the separation of natural gas components, and shows no discernible loss of separation power after performing more than 300 chromatographic separations over a period of five months. In addition a mesoscopically smooth capillary coating is obtained for HKUST-1 (Fig. 15), which does not display any cracks resulting from the drying process. The HKUST-1 column performs excellently in separating o-, $m\cdot$ -, $p$ -xylenes and ethylbenzene whose boiling points are very similar, consequently, showing that it is widely suitable for the separation of small aromatic compounds.
Besides these examples, a number of MOFs have also been developed for GC coated capillaries.104–106 For example, Zeng and co-workers reported a stationary phase of Zn-based MOF $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-lac})]$ ( ${\mathrm{bdc}}=1{,}4{\cdot}$ -benzenedicarboxylate; $\mathbf{L}{\mathsf{-l a c}}=\mathbf{L}{\mathsf{-l a c}}$ lactate) with large pores exhibiting specific recognition and selectivity for the separation of several families of biochemically important compounds with different functional groups [R–OH, ${\bf H}{\bf-C}({\bf R}){\bf=}{\bf O}.$ ${\bf C}({\bf R}{\bf R}^{\prime}){=}{\bf O}$ , H ${\scriptstyle\mathbf{IO-}}(\mathbf{R})\mathbf{C=}0$ , RO– ${\bf(R^{\prime})C}{=}{\bf O}$ , and $\mathbf{R}{\mathbf{-O-R}}^{\prime}]$ .145
MOFs have been employed to design tandem molecular sieves as a dual platform for selective solid-phase microextraction (SPME) and GC separation of target analytes in complex matrixes.148 The combination of a ZIF-8-coated fibre for SPME with a ZIF-8-coated capillary for GC allows for selective and efficient determination of volatile $n$ -alkanes in complex petroleum-based fuel and human serum samples. In addition, a combination of a ZIF-7-coated SPME fibre with an MIL-101-coated capillary column had strong power to selectively extract benzene homologues and demonstrated highresolution separation of substituted benzene derivatives because of the good selectivity of the ZIF-7 SPME fibre toward benzene homologues against linear and branched alkanes and the excellent performance of the MIL-101-coated capillary for the separation of benzene homologues and isomers of substituted benzene derivatives.
COFs have been developed for application in high-resolution gas chromatography. Yan and co-workers reported a simple and facile room-temperature solution-phase approach for the fabrication of a spherical TpBD COF (synthesized from 1,3,5-triformylphloroglucinol $\mathrm{(Tp)}$ and benzidine (BD)) with good solvent and thermal stability for GC.149 The TpBD-coated capillary column shows good ability for the separation of linear alkanes, cyclohexane and benzene, as well as double-bond positional isomers. Linear alkanes with a broad range of boiling points can be separated with their elution sequence in the order of the carbon numbers. The TpBD-coated capillary offered a high column efficiency of 4058 plates $\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ for octane and good precision for five replicate separations of alkanes. The separation of these important industrial analytes not only relies on the van der Waals interactions, but also on $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions and hydrogen bonds between analytes and the TpBD framework.
## 3.3 Capillary electrochromatography (CEC)
Owing to their permanent porosity and high stability, emerging porous materials including MOFs and COFs have found application in capillary electrochromatography. CEC is a hybrid microcolumn separation technique which combines the high selectivity of HPLC and the high efficiency of capillary electrophoresis. CEC can be carried out in packed columns where fused-silica capillaries of $50{-}100~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ I.D. are packed with small particles (typically $\leq3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\;$ , and the transport of mobile phase through CEC is achieved by an electro-osmotic flow (EOF) instead of a pressure gradient as in HPLC.120 In a typical capillary packed with silica particles the surfaces of the capillary wall and of the particles are negatively charged due to the dissociation of silanol groups (Fig. 16). Separation is achieved by partitioning between the mobile and stationary phases and by differential electrophoretic mobilities. In comparison with packed-CEC and monolithic-CEC, open tubular capillary electrochromatography (OT-CEC) shows great potential because of the ease of preparation, the absence of particle packing and frit making, as well as simple instrumental handling. For OT-CEC the inner surface is coated with a stationary phase which functions by chromatographic separation mechanisms and the mobile phase is electro-osmotically driven through the column. By applying diverse porous materials with large surface area as the stationary phase, the column capacity, phase ratio and separation selectivity may be enhanced.120 The porous materials should have good thermostability and solvent stability in electrolyte solutions and organic solvents (Table 3).
Several MOFs have been used as stationary phases for OT-CEC.64,121–127 MOF-5 was immobilized on the inner wall of the COOH-terminated capillary by Chen and co-workers.121 The COOH-terminated capillary supplies reacting points to link Zn clusters. By the treatment with a solution of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and subsequently a solution of 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid, MOF-5 deposits on the COOH-terminated capillary (Fig. 17). The capillary coated with MOF-5 exhibits exceptional separation efficiency for substituted benzene. Since ZIFs possess high surface areas, and adsorption affinities, the ZIF coating not only improves the interaction between the stationary phase and liquid molecules but also increases the phase ratio. By immobilizing ZIF-8 onto the inner surface of a silica capillary functionalized with polydopamine, ZIF-8 is applied as the stationary phase for OT-CEC while the immobilized layers are stable and have good interaction with different kinds of molecules.122 The ZIF-8 crystals immobilized on the capillary are granular and uniform. In addition to $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions and hydrophobic interactions between the stationary phase and diphenol isomers, metal ions in the crystals also enhance the interaction with the phenolic hydroxyl group, thus different positions of the phenolic hydroxyl group determine different interactions with the stationary phase, resulting in a better separation effect. The ZIF-8 bonded column performs well in separating acidic, basic, and neutral compounds, and also shows good repeatability.
A MIL-101(Cr)–polymer monolith was reported as the stationary phase for capillary electrochromatography and nano-liquid chromatography by Huang, Lin and co-workers [64]. MIL-101(Cr) is dispersed in the polymer monolith via in situ polymerization of methacrylate monomers (butyl methacrylate (BMA), ethylene dimethacrylate (EDMA) and 2-acrylamido-2-methylpropane sulphuric acid (AMPS)) with 1-hexyl-3-methylimidazolium tetrafluoroborate $([\mathbf{C_{6}m i n}][\mathbf{BF_{4}}])$ , and assisted by a 5 min microwave heating. Compared to packed columns, the MIL-101(Cr)–polymer monolith possesses larger pore sizes $(1.3~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ vs. $0.2\upmu\mathrm{m}\mathrm{\overrightarrow{\Omega}},$ with the merit of lower back pressure. The inclusion of MIL-101(Cr) provides high surface area, meso- and nano-sized pores and aromatic terephthalate moieties in the produced monolith which caused high column permeability as well as increased separation performance for all test analytes (xylene, chlorotoluene, cymene, PAHs, aromatic acids and BSA trypsin digested peptides).
COFs and porous organic cages have been immobilized on the inner wall of capillaries to achieve high capacity and phase ratio in OT-CEC.128–131 COF-5 is grown on the polydopamine modified inner wall of a capillary through the layer-by-layer (LBL) strategy via a microfluidic method.128 While another polydopamine layer may grow on the anchored COF-5 layer, which also can supply reactive sites for immobilizing COF-5, multilayer COF-5 can be formed and immobilized onto the capillary. The COF-5-coated capillary shows the ability to separate neutral compounds, acidic compounds and polar compounds with good repeatability and stability. The organic linkers of porous COF-5 contain benzene rings, allowing sorption and interaction between aromatics and the aromatic framework, while the ionized groups on the surface of the COF-5 stationary phase generate an electro-osmotic flow which produces a driving force in OT-CEC. COF-LZU1 has also been used in CEC.129 First, the high surface areas and porosity of COF-LZU1 can improve the performance of OT-CEC, while the stability can ensure that the COF-LZU1-coated column is not affected by the buffer solution. The structure of COF-LZU1 is rich in benzene ring and amino groups which provide the chemical fundamentals for improved selectivity, such as hydrophobic interactions, $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions and hydrophilic interactions between analytes and the COF-LZU1 coated open tubular column. Meanwhile, the method of in situ synthesis of COF-LZU1 in the capillary is facile and simple (Fig. 18). The inner wall of the capillary is first modified by 3-aminopropyltriethoxysilane to get a full coating of amino group, and the aldehyde group-functionalized capillary is then obtained by treating with glutaraldehyde. The building units of COF-LZU1 are rich in aldehyde and amino groups, which can serve as active sites to realize the in situ synthesis of COF-LZU1 on the inner wall of the capillary column. The capillary columns are efficient in separating neutral analytes, amino acids and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and baseline separation of these compounds is achieved with good stability and repeatability.
COF-LZU1 was used as the stationary phase in OT-CEC by being located on the inner wall of the capillary between the epoxy group of 3-glycidoxypropyltrimethoxysilane (GLYMO) and the amino group of COF-LZU1.130 To prepare the COF-LZU1- coated capillary, a bare capillary is first coated with GLYMO on its inner wall, and then filled with COF-LZU1 solution. Using the COF-LZU1-coated capillary as the stationary phase, OT-CEC shows high loading capacity, and all the model analytes including alkylbenzenes, PAHs, and anilines achieve baseline separation within $16~\mathrm{min}$ , while completely overlap when a bare capillary is used. The novel coated capillary shows a capacity of at least 300 consecutive runs, indicating its excellent stability and reproducibility.
Hypercrosslinked polymers (HCPs) have been applied to OT-CEC by Chen and co-workers.132 The silylating reagent is immobilized on the inner wall of a capillary column, and then a knitted aromatic polymer (KAP) modified capillary column is prepared through an in situ hypercrosslinking reaction. The prepared capillary column shows good separation performance for neutral compounds, small biomolecules, such as nucleosides, amino acids, small peptides, and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, sulpha drugs. The improved separation efficiency is due to the porous structure of KAPs, $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions and hydrophobic interactions between the analytes and the benzene ring in the KAP framework.
## 3.4 Chiral chromatography
A chiral pore environment is required to separate chiral isomers. Porous materials may be designed to possess chiral pore channels with a tuneable pore environment to interact with chiral guests.133 Chiral centres can be incorporated into the pores, the pore size and shape can be changed, and the functionality may provide additional supramolecular weak interactions to hold and separate enantiomers selectively. Various chiral porous materials have been used as stationary phases in HPLC, GC and CEC for enantioselective separation.
A chiral MOF-based HPLC column was reported by Nuzhdin and co-workers.89 3D porous homochiral Zn-MOF $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})\cdot$ (L-lac)(DMF)] DMF with intrinsic chirality was used as a chiral stationary phase for enantioselective liquid chromatography. (R)- and (S)-enantiomers of alkyl aryl sulfoxides are obtained by chromatographic resolution of the racemic mixtures of sulfoxides. For column chromatographic separations, a glass tube of $8\mathrm{mm}$ inner diameter was charged with a suspension of Zn-MOF to obtain a $33\mathrm{\cm}$ high column. While electron-withdrawing substituents reduce the coordinating ability of the sorbate and thus reduce the sorption constants, introduction of electron-donating substituents results in higher sorption constants but a lower stereoselectivity factor which leads to poorer enantioseparation. Zn-MOF also possesses catalytic activity for the oxidation of corresponding sulphides with $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ . By combining both chromatographic and catalytic properties of Zn-MOF, a unique one-pot process has been developed for the synthesis of enantiomerically pure sulfoxides.
Open tubular columns coated with chiral porous materials have been used in the high-resolution GC separation of chiral compounds. A three-dimensional chiral MOF, $[\{\mathrm{cu(sala)}\}_{n}]$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{sala}=N{-}\left(2\right.$ -hydroxybenzyl)-L-alanine), was used as the stationary phase and chromatograms show baseline or at least $85\%$ valley separation for enantiomer pairs including citronellal, phenylsuccinic acid, 1-phenyl-1,2-ethandiol, and 1-phenylethanol.109 A $\mathrm{Co(\mathrm{p\mathrm{-}C a m)_{1/2}(\mathrm{bdc)_{1/2}(\mathrm{tmdpy)}}}}$ $\left(\mathbf{D}{\cdot}\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{am}}\right.=\mathbf{\Phi}_{\mathbf{D}}.$ camphoric acid; bdc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate; $\operatorname{tmdpy}=4,4^{\prime}.$ -trimethylenedipyridine)- coated open tubular column was fabricated for high-resolution GC separation of citronellal, limonene, glutamic acid, proline, 1-phenylethanol, 1-phenyl-1,2-ethanediol, and 2-amino-1-butanol.110 Chiral imine-based COFs including CTpPa-1, CTpPa-2 and CTpBD were developed to fabricate chiral COF-bound capillary columns via an in situ growth approach for chiral separation (Fig. 19).111 All the chiral COFs distributed uniformly on the inner wall of the capillary show a crystalline eclipsed AA structure with fluffy aggregation of a sheet-like structure, and possess large BET surface area and pore size. The chiral COF-bound capillary columns demonstrate high resolution for the separation of enantiomers such as $(\pm){-}1$ -phenylethanol, $(\pm)$ -1-phenyl-1-propanol, $(\pm)\cdot$ -limonene and $(\pm)\cdot$ -methyl lactate with excellent repeatability and reproducibility.
For OT-CEC separation, a homochiral MOF coated capillary column has been developed using $z\mathrm{no}$ nanoparticles as efficient nucleating agents for in situ rapid synthesis of a homochiral MOF $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{s}\cdot\mathrm{nip})_{2}]_{n}$ $(\operatorname{s-nip}\ =\ (S)-2\cdot$ (1,8-naphthalimido)-3-(4-imidazole)- propanoate) in the capillary inner wall.123 The use of nucleating agents in the MOF preparing process significantly reduces the synthesis time, and the method allows in situ synthesis of homochiral $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{s}\cdot\mathrm{nip})_{2}]_{n}$ in the capillary inner surface in $^{\mathrm{~1~h~}}$ (Fig. 20). Good enantioseparation of three monoamine neurotransmitters is achieved, and the isomers of nitrophenols and analogues of bisphenols can also be well separated.
Homochiral porous organic cages have been used as chromatographic stationary phases by coating them inside a standard capillary column. The CC3-R-coated capillary columns can be used for the GC separation of a range of mixtures including aromatic compounds, racemic mixtures, and branched alkanes using molecular sieving.112 In contrast a CC9-coated capillary column using imine-linked POC CC9 as the stationary phase has good complementarity to the $\upbeta$ -DEX 120 column and CC3- $R$ column for enantioseparation.113 The capillary column exhibits excellent enantioseparation for various racemates, and positional isomers were also efficiently separated on the capillary column. CC3- $R$ diluted with polysiloxane (OV-1701) was also used as stationary phase for high-resolution GC with excellent enantioselectivity.114 A large number of optical isomers are resolved without derivatization. In addition, CC3-R also exhibits good selectivity for the separation of isomers, linear alkanes, alcohols, and aromatic hydrocarbons. The imine-linked POC CC10 diluted with polysiloxane OV-1701 was also explored as a novel stationary phase for high-resolution GC.115 A wide variety of enantiomers belonging to different classes of organic compounds can be resolved on the coated capillary column. In addition, CC10 also exhibits good selectivity for the separation of $n$ -alkanes, $n$ -alcohols, Grob mixture, and positional isomers. The fabricated column represents a chiral recognition complementarity in comparison with the commercial $\upbeta$ -DEX 120 column and CC3- $R$ column.
## 4. Porous materials for continuous flow catalytic synthesis
Continuous flow systems have been extensively studied for pharmaceutical and fine chemical manufacturing, and sustainable chemical production.134–137 They are receiving increasing attention as an alternative to conventional batch reactions due to their precise control over reaction parameters and improved safety regimes.138,139 Flow reactors are based on microfluidic principles and typically employ enclosed microchannel structures of tube networks that are on the order of 50–1000 microns in diameter. The microchannel structures of these reactors lead to resultantly small internal volumes often in the range of $0.1{-}10\mathrm{mL}$ . Continuous flow synthesis is typically conducted by mechanically pumping reactant solutions through a passive mixer unit (often a simple T-piece) followed by a residence segment of channel or tubing. Increasingly, such flow reactors are constructed from readily available parts, such as small diameter PTFE (polytetrafluoroethylene) tubing and micro-mixers.
Homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysis as well as biocatalysis have been employed in flow reactors. Microchannel reactors with catalyst immobilization combine both reaction and separation when performed in a continuous-flow mode. The reaction and separation of supported catalysts and products can be performed simultaneously when heterogeneous catalysts are packed in continuous-flow columns. The lack of mechanical stirring or agitation in a flow mode avoids mechanical degradation of the packed material. The very high surface-to-volume ratio improves heat and mass transfer and good contact between reagents and the catalyst created by an exceptionally high local concentration of the catalyst. With flow systems it is often straightforward to control the feed of reactant solutions into the reactor, reaction temperature, mixing regime, and the residence time/reaction time.140 Microchannel reactors can display much higher activity than batch reactors due to good accessibility to the active sites by reagents and the high interfacial area.141,142 Microfluidic devices also provide the possibility of carrying out reactions more safely under more forcing reaction conditions. Moreover, there are other advantages such as easy automation, easy scale-up and reducibility, integrated inline/online analysis and multistep transformation with unstable intermediates. When porous materials are used for heterogeneous catalysts of microchannel reactors, low catalyst loading is expected and the anchored catalyst in porous materials promotes catalytic activity and ensures good stability and recyclability. In this section porous materials for continuous flow catalytic synthesis are discussed on the basis of three main approaches, (1) packed columns, (2) monolithic columns and (3) open tubular columns, in particular in microreactors (Fig. 21).142–145
## 4.1. Packed columns
Porous materials have been employed as catalysts in microflow reactors with packed columns. In the packed-bed reactors, channels are filled with catalyst-supported porous materials and the reaction solution is flowed through the porous materials. Porous materials have been shown to be good candidates to support molecular146,147 or nanocatalysts.148,149
HKUST-1 ground particles were used in a packed-bed reactor for the acetalization reaction of benzaldehyde with methanol into 1,1-dimethoxytoluene,150 showing a considerable enhancement of catalytic performance. Composite pellets of the HKUST-1@silica aerogel were prepared by the coupled sol–gel and water-in-oil emulsion method using supercritical $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ drying used as a catalyst for isomerisation of styrene oxide to phenyl acetaldehyde in a continuous flow reactor.151 The packed-bed cartridge-like reactor with HKUST-1@silica aerogel composite pellets was used for the isomerisation of styrene oxide to phenyl acetaldehyde.
Pd nanoparticles have been supported on MOFs/COFs for continuous-flow synthesis. Pd nanoparticles $(8~\mathrm{wt\%})$ supported on MIL-101-NH2 were reported by Martı´n-Matute and co-workers,152 and the catalyst was packed in an Omnifit glass column for the synthesis of heteroatom-functionalized biaryls through Suzuki– Miyaura cross-coupling under continuous flow. Pd nanoparticles were trapped within COF-300 nanopores, and the resulting catalyst $\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}@\mathrm{COF}{-}300$ was packed in an Omnifit glass column with glass beads ( $\cdot2\ \mathrm{mm})$ for continuous-flow Suzuki–Miyaura crosscoupling.153 An interesting example was reported by Zou, Martı´n-Matute and co-workers to show that Pd nanoparticles were stabilized and protected by a double-layered $\mathbf{MOF}@$ silica support to get the supported catalyst $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL}\ –88\mathbf{B}\ –\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}@\mathrm{r}$ ano$\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ (Fig. 22).154 Pd metallic nanoparticles are first impregnated in the pores of MIL- ${\cdot88\mathbf{B-N}\mathbf{H}_{2}}$ to obtain $\mathbf{Pd}\textcircled{\scshape A M I L-88B-N H_{2}}$ ( $8\mathrm{wt\%}$ Pd). The MOF catalyst is subsequently enclosed in a protective coating of $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles to produce a more resistant material. The catalytic performance of $\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{@MIL-88B-NH_{2}(\mathrm{2nano-SiO_{2}}}$ was evaluated in a packed-bed reactor consisting of a frittedglass chromatography column for aerobic oxidation of benzylic alcohols to their corresponding carbonyl compounds using only air as the oxidant, at ambient pressure without using any bubbling device. 1-Phenylethanol was chosen as the model substrate, and the material can endure more than 7 d of continuous operation with stable conversion at ca. $80\%$ in a microflow reactor.
A chiral MOF has been shown as a heterogeneous chiral Lewis acid catalyst for continuous-flow synthesis with a packed column by Cui and co-workers.155 The chiral MOF is built from dimanganese units and tetracarboxylate ligands derived from chiral $^{1,1^{\prime}}$ -biphenol-derived phosphoric acids and catalyzes enantioselective Friedel–Crafts alkylation of $N\mathrm{.}$ -methylindole with $\{\},\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -unsaturated $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -ketoesters under continuous-flow conditions in a packed-bed reactor (Fig. 23).
## 4.2. Monolithic columns
Macroporous monolithic materials have been used as solid supports for catalysts and reagents in many studies. Monolithic polymers and silica are the most common supports and have been applied in flow catalysis.143,156 There are a number of reports about polymer monolith-supported catalytic microreactors. The polymer monoliths have been used as supporting materials for immobilization of various homogeneous catalysts.156,157 Immobilization of transition metal catalysts has been investigated such as Pd-based Heck’s catalysts,158 Schrock’s catalysts,159 Grubbs catalyst (Fig. 24),160–164 and Hoveyda-type catalyst based on flourocarboxylates.164 Monolith-supported chiral catalysts have been reported for chiral amino alcohol catalysts,165 dynamic kinetic resolution,166,167 and chiral PyBox ligands.168,169 In addition polymer monoliths have been used for the immobilization of biocatalysts, for example, trypsin.170 However, functionalized polymer monoliths have only interparticle porosity and small surface areas due to the lack of microporosity.171–173
In contrast to the polymer monoliths, silica monoliths are hierarchical materials to support catalysts for use in flow synthesis. For example, aminopropylated silica has been used for Knoevenagel condensation.174 Silica monoliths were also developed for highly active and selective catalysts in the total and preferential oxidation of carbon monoxide.175 Moreover, silica monoliths were developed for biocatalyst immobilization, e.g., lipase immobilization for triglyceride transesterification.176
As an important type of catalyst, metallic nanoparticle catalysts have been anchored to a polymer matrix or loaded onto the porosity of silica.177 For example, Pd nanoparticles were developed in silica monoliths for hydrogenation of styrene178 and selective hydrogenation of 3-hexyn-1-ol.178,179 Silica monoliths functionalized with Au nanoparticles in the range of $6.8\pm1.4\mathrm{{nm}}$ were used for the selective oxidation of cyclohexene under constant flow at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ 180
Monolithic MOF porous materials have been rarely used in continuous catalytic flow reactors so far. Galarneau and co-workers first reported an example to combine the catalytic properties of HKUST-1 with the high mass transport efficiency of silica monoliths.181 HKUST-1 is introduced as nanoparticles inside the mesoporosity of a silica monolith with hierarchical macro-/mesoporosity (Fig. 25). The heterogeneous HKUST-1/ silica composite monolith catalyst shows high productivity for the continuous-flow Friedla¨nder reaction between 2-aminobenzophenone and acetylacetone. The productivity at the steady state is
0.5 mmol $\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ gMonolith1 or $2.2\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}\ \mathrm{g_{CuBTC}}^{-1}$ , which is 2.5 times more productive than the commercial HKUST-1 powder. An example of combining the catalytic property of ZIF-8 with polymer monoliths was shown by Maya and co-workers.182 In a microfluidic flow-through reactor ZIF-8 coating of pores in a monolithic polymer support is active for heterogeneous catalysis in the Knoevenagel condensation of benzaldehyde and ethyl cyanoacetate.
## 4.3. Open tubular columns
For open tubular or wall-coated microreactors, supported catalysts are coated on the capillary wall. Coating of porous materials significantly enlarges the surface area of the microchannel walls, making such microreactors attractive in catalysis, especially in heterogeneously catalyzed multiple-phase reactions (e.g., gas– liquid–solid reactions).183 The internal surface of the fused silica capillary of microreactors is readily functionalized with mesoporous inorganic thin films, e.g., titania, to support various metallic nanoparticle catalysts.184–188 For example, a mesoporous $\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ thin film with $200\ \mathrm{nm}$ thickness was fabricated in capillary microreactors to immobilize Pd nanoparticles for the hydrogenation of phenylacetylene to realize the order of reaction, $n=0.6$ , rate constant, $k=0.2085$ , and activation energy, $E=41.36\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ .189 Thin films of mesoporous anodic aluminium oxide materials can also be used for the deposition of ca. $2.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ metallic nanoparticles of NiPd, FePd, MgPd, Pd, Pt, and Au for organic transformations, e.g., the reduction of 4-nitrophenol with Au nanoparticles.190
Open tubular capillary columns with supported gel catalysts coated on the capillary wall have been developed.74,191 Gel catalysts have high hierarchical porosity, a highly tuneable and unique molecular environment and are a promising type of supported catalyst. To assemble a microfluidic reactor coated with a dynamic covalent chiral gel, capillaries are first modified with amine functional groups on the inner wall, then a Mn-salen imine chiral gel is introduced via in situ gelation and is anchored on the inner wall via imine bonding forming a layer of catalytically active gel matrix (Fig. 7).74 The Mn-salen gel has proven effective for the enantioselective kinetic resolution of secondary alcohols, giving good conversions and an enantiomeric excess that optimal conversions were achieved in a much shorter reaction time $(15\ \mathrm{min})$ compared to the batch process $\left(120\mathrm{\min}\right)$ (Fig. 26). Moreover, the capillary reactor can be reused without loss of activity or enantioselectivity. Later porphyrin-based imine gels impregnated with metal ions were used to assemble a catalytic microfluidic reactor,192 which had sponge-like porous networked structures consisting of interconnected nanoparticles with hierarchical porosity, and possessed high specific surface areas (up to $719\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and large pore volumes (up to $2.60~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . A catalytic microfluidic capillary reactor is assembled with a Pd–porphyrin imine gel coating for Suzuki–Miyaura crosscoupling reactions, and similar yields can be achieved in a much shorter reaction time compared to the batch processes. A polymer brush gel has also been grown on the interior of a glass microreactor to immobilize in situ formed silver and palladium nanoparticles by Vancso, Verboom and co-workers.193 The polymer brush gel film is grown by means of atom-transfer radical polymerization. The metal nanoparticles are stable during the reduction of 4-nitrophenol in microreactors.
A microfluidic reactor was fabricated by growing Au nanoparticles (NPs) supported by imidazolium-based porous organic polymers (IM-POPs) along the inner surface of a fused-silica capillary.194 $\mathrm{\Delta\mathrm{{ACl}_{4}}^{-}}$ anions are exchanged with $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}^{-}$ due to the cationic nature of IM-POPs, and Au NPs are formed and stabilized within the porous channels of the IM-POP layer through an in situ reduction induced by $\mathrm{{NaBH_{4}}}$ solution (Fig. 27). The thickness of the hybrid material layers is tuneable by changing the amounts of injected reactants. The Au/IM-POP layers grown with the microfluidic capillary exhibit significantly higher catalytic efficiency in terms of TOF $(6161~\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ than bulk Au/IM-POPs $(647\ \mathrm{h}^{-1})$ , suggesting that the capillary confinement plays a crucial role in increasing the conversion rate of each catalytic site and possibly reducing the consumption of noble metal catalysts.
Several MOF materials have been coated onto silica capillaries for continuous flow reactions. $\mathbf{NO}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–101(\mathrm{Cr})$ was chemically modified with chelating groups that could bind ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions and used to catalyze the 1,3-dipolar cycloaddition of benzylazide to phenylacetylene in a capillary microreactor.195 Pd nanoparticles were immobilized in a capillary quartz microreactor coated with a UiO- $66\substack{-\Delta\mathbf{V}\mathbf{H}_{2}}$ film.196 3-Aminopropyltriethoxysilane is first employed to chemically modify the inner surface of the quartz capillary, and a continuous $\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ film is grown on the inner surface, followed by immobilization of Pd nanoparticles inside the UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ film assisted by the amino groups. The Pd/UiO$66\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ film quartz capillary microreactor retains a conversion of approximately $100\%$ for the reduction of 4-nitrophenol during the continuous flow reaction even for $^{100\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
Besides the above three types of catalytic microreactors, several other reactors have been reported. Positioning and anchoring of MOF catalysts can be achieved in microreactors using a magnet.197,198 $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\textcircled{\mathrm{a}}\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ magnetic core–shell microspheres are synthesized using the encapsulation technique based on poly(styrenesulfonate, sodium salt) coating of Fe3O4 particles.197 $\mathbf{Fe}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ magnetic nanoparticles are used as supports for the ZIF-8 catalyst, which can then be separated from the reaction medium by simple application of a magnet. The resulting $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ is used as a catalyst in a tubular flow microreactor for Knoevenagel condensation between benzaldehyde and ethyl cyanoacetate. The catalytic activity arises from ZIF-8 where the 2-methylimidazole ligand acts as an efficient base catalyst. The performance of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ is better in a flow reactor than under batch conditions ( $100\%$ vs. $50\%$ conversion after $25\mathrm{min}$ ). A catalyst can be installed on a membrane at the microchannel centre.199,200 Uozumi and co-workers showed that a Pd–poly(4-vinylpyridine)polymer membrane (thickness $10~{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ forms at the interface between two laminar precursor flows inside microchannels. Subsequent reduction yielded a polymeric Pd–nanoparticle memebraneinstalled microreactor (Fig. 28). The microreactor facilitated instantaneous quantitative conversion (residence time 2–8 s) in the hydrodehalogenation of aryl chlorides, bromides, iodides and triflates.
## 5. Porous materials for microfluidic membrane fabrication
Membrane technologies using emerging porous materials have been developed to enhance the membrane selectivity and permeability. The membranes of porous materials prepared on inorganic or polymeric tubular or hollow fibre supports represent a high degree of process intensification for scaling-up.201–206 Such fibre membrane systems have thin selective layers supported on low-cost supports and are advantageous for large-scale separation processes.207 In comparison with the above three capillary column strategies, the microchannel supports are permeable and porous membranes can be synthesized either on the inner surface or on the outer surface of the supports for promising performance in a variety of gas or liquid mixture separations (Fig. 29). To achieve porous membranes with good attachment to the support surfaces, surface modification with polymeric coatings or organosilane reagents or seeding procedures combined with secondary growth have been employed to enhance the adhesion and growth of porous membranes (Table 4).
A static hydro-/solvothermal crystallization synthesis method has been used to support MOF continuous membranes mainly on inorganic hollow fibres, such as hollow alumina and other ceramic fibres,208–216 because the high thermal stabilities of ceramic supports can sustain the hydro-/solvothermal conditions for MOF growth. Coating of the surface with a thin metal oxide layer (e.g., ZnO) via a sol–gel method217 or a manual-rubbing deposition218 method efficiently promotes the hydro-/solvothermal growth of the MOF membrane. Dip-coating of a $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sol was also reported to induce the growth of a MOF $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ membrane on the inner surface of a tubular ${\tt a}{\tt a}{\tt A}_{2}{\tt O}_{3}$ support.219 Furthermore, a ${\bf Z}{\bf n}{\bf O}{-}{\bf A l}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ composite hollow fibre was prepared in one step to avoid the extra procedure for $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ coating resulting in the in situ synthesis of ZIF-8 membranes on the external surface of composite hollow fibers.220 The resulting ZIF-8 membrane exhibited excellent hydrogen permeance in the order of magnitude of $\times10^{-6}\mathrm{\mol\m}^{-2}\mathrm{\s}^{-1}\mathrm{\Pa}^{-1}$ and good selectivity up to 12 for $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ over ${\bf N}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . Continuous HKUST-1 membranes were deposited on the surface of polyvinylidene fluoride hollow fibres through a pressure-assisted seed-free growth process by using copper hydroxide nanostrand thin films as the copper source at RT.221 In another strategy, hollow fibres are pre-modified chemically for subsequent deposition of MOF membranes.222–225 Polyvinylidene fluoride hollow fibre can be ammoniated by ethanediamine for adhesive growth of a thin and continuous ${\bf N H}_{2}–\bf M I L}–53$ layer $(c a.8\upmu\mathrm{m})$ on the outer surface of an ammoniated hollow fibre.222 Continuous HKUST-1 films were fabricated on pre-seeded ${\ttalpha}\mathrm{-}{\tt A l}_{2}{\tt O}_{3}$ hollow fibres modified with chitosan, which is abundant of amino and hydroxyl groups and is used to improve the binding of the HKUST-1 memebrane.223
Flow synthesis is an effective approach to prepare MOF membranes especially on the inner surface. Flow synthesis increased the heterogeneous nucleation of MOF crystals and the formation of continuous MOF membranes, thus leading to high performance MOF membranes. A flow direct in situ synthesis method was developed to fabricate a continuous ZIF-8 membrane on the inner surface of an ${\ttalpha}\mathrm{-}{\tt A l}_{2}{\tt O}_{3}$ hollow fibre.226 The $\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{\alpha}$ hollow fibre is pre-treated with 3-aminopropyltriethoxysilane to covalently link the membrane resulting in a thin and continuous ZIF-8 membrane $(c a.\ 2\ \upmu\mathrm{m})$ .227 A continuous ZIF-8 film was also grown on the inner surface of a quartz capillary using 3-aminopropyltriethoxysilane as a covalent linker.228
Polymeric hollow fibres are much cheaper than ceramic ones and widely used while they have low thermal stability and organic resistance at high temperature. Fluidic processing techniques operating under low temperature and mild conditions thus are preferred in MOF membrane fabrication on polymeric hollow fibres.229 ZIF-7 and ZIF-8 continuous membranes have been supported on the inner surface of a polysulfone hollow fibre with $520~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ outer diameter using microfluidics (Fig. 30).230 With the use of microfluidics, crystal growth is limited to the inner surface of the support, while a constant reagent concentration is maintained over time and along the membrane length. This provides a constant reactant concentration along the membrane for growing homogeneous, continuous and thin layers of ZIF-7 $(2.4~{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ and ZIF-8 $(3.6~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ with good adherence. The high quality of the membranes and the zeolite-like sodalite porous structure of both MOFs with pore apertures of $3.0{-}3.4\mathring\mathrm{A}$ proved to be extraordinarily selective in the separation of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ containing binary mixtures, and allowed operation at high temperature $(150~^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ and pressure (4 bar).
Highly selective ZIF-8 membranes have been fabricated on the inner surface of polyamide-imide hollow fibres via the interfacial microfluidic membrane processing technique by Nair and co-workers.231,232 The fabrication of ZIF-8 membranes can be controlled by processing parameters (reactant flow profiles, concentrations, temperature profiles) as well as the microstructures of the hollow fibre supports. Interfacial membrane formation in the hollow fibre occurs via an initial formation of two distinct layers and the subsequent rearrangement into a single layer. The membrane morphology is uniform, with an average thickness of $8.1\pm1.6\upmu\mathrm{m}$ along the fibre length. Although ZIF-8 membranes can separate $\mathbf{C}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{6}$ from $\mathbf{C}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{8}$ by virtue of diffusivity differences, they often lose their selectivity at higher pressures. Interestingly, the membranes fabricated through the microfluidic technique show excellent performance characteristics at elevated feed pressures of up to 9.5 bar, maintaining a $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ separation factor of 90 and $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ flux as high as 4 mmol $\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ at 9.5 bar feed pressure.
ZIF-8 membranes were also processed in polyamide-imide hollow fibres via a interfacial microfluidic membrane processing technique combining a two-solvent interfacial approach for positional control over membrane formation (at inner and outer surfaces, or in the bulk, of the fibres).233 As shown in Fig. 31, a dilute $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ solution is flowed through the bore of a horizontally mounted Torlons polyamide-imide fibre, and a concentrated 2-methylimidazole aqueous solution is present in the reactor chamber on the shell side. To suppress the membrane bypass, a lumen-capping step is accomplished by applying a controlled amount of a poly(dimethylsiloxane) solution to the ends of the mounted module. Due to the relatively rapid transport of reactants to the interface under continuous flow, a continuous membrane of average thickness $8.8\pm1.4\mathrm{{mm}}$ forms throughout the fibre length. The ZIF-8 membrane shows high $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}$ and $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ separation factors $370\pm60$ at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $12\pm3$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ , respectively). Moreover, these ZIF-8 membranes remain stable after long-term permeation measurements, even over one month of testing.
ZIF-8 and ZIF-93 continuous membranes are grown on the inner side of polyimide hollow fibre supports by microfluidics, leading to continuous supported membranes.234–237 A $2.6~\pm$ $0.4~\mathrm{mm}$ continuous intergrowth ZIF-93 layer is shown by SEM, strongly attached on the inner surface of the $\mathbf{P}84^{\mathbb{R}}$ polyimide hollow fibre, with an uninterrupted wide interface between the two materials indicating a strong affinity. High selectivities in the $\mathrm{{H}}_{2}/\mathrm{{CH}}_{4}$ (59.7) and $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ (16.9) mixtures at $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ were obtained. A thermal annealing process carried out after the MOF synthesis can improve MOF growth and subsequent separation performance of the membranes.235 This is due to the enhancement of the affinity between the polymer support and MOF when the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ZIF}({\widehat{\mathbf{a}}})$ polyimide hollow fibre membrane is annealed in situ at $175^{\circ}\mathrm{{^C}}$ below the glass transition temperature. This procedure improves the interaction (i.e. the MOF–polymer adherence) via a synergistic rearrangement of the polymer chains in the presence of ZIF particles forming continuous layers (characterized by SEM), leading to an increase in the $\mathrm{{H}}_{2}/\mathrm{{CH}_{4}}$ separation selectivity without any permeation rate loss.
Ultrathin ZIF-8 membranes of ca. $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ thickness were synthesized on the inner side of Matrimids polymeric hollow fibres by a seeded growth.236 The seed layers of ZIF-8 were rapidly formed under microwave irradiation. Subsequently, continuous defect-free ZIF-8 membranes were formed by flowing precursor solution through the seeded hollow fibres. The membranes exhibited an average propylene/propane separation factor of $\sim46$ and average propylene permeance of ${\sim}183~\times~10^{-10}\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{m}^{-2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\ \mathrm{Pa}^{-1}$ or 55 GPU (permeability $\sim49.4$ Barrer).
Growth of continuous defect-free ZIF-8 thin films on the outer surface of polyamide-imide hollow fibres was investigated as well.235 ZIF-8 is grown on polyamide-imide using continuous flow synthesis, where an aqueous $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ solution is flowed on the shell side of the hollow fibre, and an aqueous 2-methylimidazole solution is flowed through the bore as shown in Fig. 32. The gas separation was evaluated for post-combustion carbon capture, and these membranes demonstrate a $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ permeance of 22 GPU and the highest reported $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity of 52.
## 6. Porous materials for microfluidic devices
Porous materials have been applied in devices. Microfluidic devices can be prepared by lithographic patterning or other microfabrication techniques to produce intricate nano/microchannels through which reagent solutions are passed and mixed in various conformations.138,238 Integration of porous materials into devices requires the materials to be located onto different substrates (e.g., silicon, metal, glass, and flexible polymers). Unlike microchannels, microfluidic devices have advantages of portability, the capacity for integration, precise control over solid phase structure, and precise control over reaction processes. Microfluidic systems are more efficient with small volumes of fluids, and have been applied in separation, catalysis and analytical science.239
Precise positioning of porous materials is important for improving the performance and lowering the cost of microelectronic devices.240–242 Digital microfluidics is a powerful technique in printing large arrays of monodisperse crystals with highthroughput, high flexibility, and low cost in a short time period. The technique enables small droplets $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ to nL-sized) of solution to be independently manipulated on the surface of a hydrophobic substrate using software controlled electronic signals (Fig. 33), which makes it well suited to print porous material patterns on a range of substrates.243,244
Digital microfluidics has been demonstrated to accurately deposit monodisperse individual MOF (HKUST-1) crystals by Lammertyn and co-workers,245 thereby enabling high-throughput, yet flexible single crystal patterning. The hydrophobic surface of the device is patterned to obtain hydrophilic-in-hydrophobic micropatches onto which thousands of femtoliter droplets of the MOF precursor solution are printed per second in a chosen pattern. The proposed method has been proved successful in the printing of HKUST-1 crystals. A mother droplet of the HKUST-1 precursor solution is dispensed from an on-chip fluid reservoir and transported over an array of $20~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\times20~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ micropatches of ITO in a Teflon-AF background matrix (Fig. 34). These droplets can be routed over a desired electrode path by applying a $150\mathrm{V}$ actuation voltage in a sequential fashion. Droplet actuation is achieved by the electrowetting-on-dielectric principle, in which an imbalance in the interfacial tension between a liquid droplet and an electrode coated with a dielectric layer is used to propel the droplet. After printing, the top plate is removed from the device and placed inside a Petri dish containing a droplet of DMSO to ensure slow evaporation of the latter. The femtoliter droplets are allowed to evaporate at room temperature. Compared to other methods, this technology allows the flexible fabrication of huge arrays of monodisperse MOF crystals with high parallelism, and does not rely on any expensive equipment. SEM images demonstrate a very high single-crystal per micropatch resolution and welldefined single octahedral crystals inside the hydrophilic micropatches. The same research group also showed that digital microfluidics can scale down and automate the layer-by-layer process to deposit patterned MOF (HKUST-1) coatings.246
Multichannel integrated microfluidic devices with porous polymer monoliths were developed for automated preterm birth biomarker analysis by Sahore and co-workers.247 Each microfluidic chip had the top two layers made from poly(dimethylsiloxane) and the bottom two layers constructed from a cyclic olefin copolymer (COC). The top layer has through-holes that connected COC to poly(dimethylsiloxane) and with the bottom layer having a channel where the reversed-phase porous polymer monolith was formed. The octyl methacrylate reversed-phase porous polymer monolith $\left(0.6\ \mathrm{mm\long}\right)$ inside the channel was fabricated inside the COC microfluidic channel. The monolith was used to preconcentrate the biomarkers through solid-phase extraction for subsequent analysis.
MOFs have been fabricated in microreactors of copper foils/ stainless steel plates with microchannels for separation and catalysis. A HKUST-1 layer with an average thickness of $5{\mathrm{-}}15\upmu\mathrm{m}$ has also been grown inside a copper sheet containing microchannels via an electrochemical synthesis method by Denayer and co-workers.248 Methanol and $n$ -hexane vapours were separated on the microdevice in breakthrough experiments. Faster adsorption rates and low pressure drop were observed for the microseparator compared to a conventional packed bed. Growing of ZIF-8 films in a microreactor of copper foils with microchannels was achieved by Zamaro and co-workers.249 Ag was subsequently immobilized in the MOF film for gas-phase reactions. The microreactor showed high stability in the carbon monoxide oxidation reaction.
A composite layer of ZIF-8 and NaA zeolite was grown in a multichannel porous stainless steel plate for continuous flow reactions.250 The NaA zeolite membrane (ca. $1\ \upmu\mathrm{m})$ was first introduced on the porous stainless steel microchannels. Subsequently a ZnO layer was introduced as a precursor seed layer and a continuous ZIF-8 membrane was grown over the NaA zeolite layer (Fig. 35). The membrane microreactor was applied in the Knoevenagel condensation of benzaldehyde and ethyl cyanoacetate with $99\%$ product yield in $20~\mathrm{min}$ residence time and long-term stability of over $50\mathrm{~h~}$ .
Porous materials have been applied in a number of enzymebased catalytic microfluidic devices. A microfluidic device with an enzyme catalyst immobilized in polymer porous materials has been developed by Hernandez and co-workers (Fig. 36).251
The reservoir contains a porous surface with enzymatic catalytic activity provided by the reversible immobilization of horseradish peroxidase onto the copolymer of polystyrene and poly(styrene-cocyclodextrin based styrene) (P(S-coSCD)). The pores enriched in cyclodextrin are employed to immobilize horseradish peroxidase (previously modified with adamantane) by host–guest interactions between the cyclodextrin units and adamantine groups. Honeycomb patterned porous films with $\sim3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ pore diameter are obtained in this way. Additionally, the films prepared using P(S-coSCD) as an additive show localized fluorescent signals within the pores, indicating that the host–guest interactions are localized in the pore surface. While enzymes exhibit limited solution stability and is hard to recycle, these surfaces exhibit catalytic activity that remains stable during several reaction cycles. Moreover, the porous platforms can be recovered by using free water-soluble $\upbeta\mathrm{.}$ -CD with detergents.
Digital microfluidic reactors with immobilized enzymes were employed to study cytochrome P450 (CYP)-mediated drug metabolism on the droplet scale by Sathyanarayanan and coworkers.252 The on-chip reactors consist of thiol–ene porous polymer monolith plugs functionalized with recombinant $\mathbf{CYP_{1}A_{1}}$ isoforms (Fig. 37). A thiol–ene mixture of tetra-thiol and tri-ene was pipetted on top of each hydrophilic spot of the digital microfluidic bottom plate, and cured under UV (through the glass plate). The monoliths were sequentially functionalized with fluorescent-derivatized streptavidin and fluorescent-labeled (lissamine rhodamine B) biotinylated recombinant $\mathbf{CYP_{1}A_{1}}$ supersomes. The devices also incorporate inkjet-printed microheaters for on-demand regio-specific heating, and a custom fluorescence quantification method was developed for detection of the chosen $\mathbf{CYP_{1}A_{1}}$ model activity. Ethoxyresorufin-O-deethylation was chosen as a model reaction. Ten parallel devices provided statistically significant differences in fluorescence response compared to any of the negative controls with an average $\left(n=10\right)$ turnover rate of $20.3\pm9.0$ fmol resorufin per minute.
A microfluidic device with an enzyme catalyst immobilized in ZIF-8 has been employed to develop a biosensing diagnostic device by Razmjou, Chen and co-workers.253 Polydopamine/ polyethyleneimine (PDA/PEI) is employed to pattern enzyme– MOF in microfluidic channels on a hydrophobic polypropylene (PP) membrane and provides liquid pumpless transportation. ZIF-8, glucose oxidase and horseradish peroxidase enzymes are in situ grown on the PDA/PEI patterning. ZIF-8 is crystallized with embedded enzymes in the robust enzyme-MOF thin films. Due to the presence of ZIF-8, the composites on PDA/PEI patterns have good acid and thermal stability and can weaken the effect of enzyme activity reduction in an adverse environment. The device shows high selectivity toward glucose with a linear sensitivity of 0.00303 Abs per $\upmu\mathbf{M}$ , and the limit of detection of ${8\ \upmu\mathbf{M}}$ glucose concentration. ZIF-8 contributes to the linear sensitivity via a diffusion limiting effect.
## 7. Conclusions and perspective
Despite the relative immaturity of the field, the combination of porous materials and capillary columns/microfluidics is demonstrating enormous promise. The areas that have experienced most growth and research are analytical chromatography, catalysis and membrane separations. In fact, considerable progress and innovation has already been achieved for analytical separation methods such as high-performance liquid chromatography, gas chromatography and capillary electrochromatography using a variety of emerging porous materials. There are now numerous examples of these materials proving to be highly effective stationary phases that enable the efficient separation of organic molecules. A number of these materials have also proven to have high thermal and chemical stability which permits their prolonged and repeated use for analytical separations. A range of transition metal-based catalysts have been immobilized onto porous materials and applied to continuous flow reactions. These continuous flow reactors can be divided into three major categories based on their immobilization method, namely: packed-bed, monolith and coated-wall reactors. They have been demonstrated to promote a number of chemical transformations, facilitating catalyst separation from products and also proving to be recyclable over numerous runs.
To date, only a limited number of MOFs, COFs, and porous organic cages, among the huge diversity of emerging porous materials, have been selected for further investigations within microfluidic environments. Besides the rich pool of known porous materials, the next generation of novel porous materials with new functionalities will drive further research interest in this area producing new innovations and applications. The majority of research to date has focused on analytical chromatography and separation via membranes. These areas will continue to grow and provide further opportunities owing to their industrial importance. For analytical chromatography there are still many challenges to be tackled if these emerging porous materials are going to compete with materials that are already established as stationary phases in the industry. For example, there needs to be further demonstrations of effective separation of complex organic molecules, more research into their stabilities and longevity, a greater understanding of the design features that effect good separation (pore sizes, hydrophobicity etc.) and side-by-side comparisons with industrial standards. Porous materials that have been specifically designed and/or functionalized to facilitate chromatographic separations will also provide key challenges in this area and will certainly arise over the coming years.
Practical applications of porous materials in chromatography form an interesting topic. For HPLC the stationary phase should have sufficient mechanical strength to stand high pressure, good solvent stability in the mobile phase (such as water/methanol or water/acetonitrile in RP-HPLC, nonpolar organic solvents in NP-HPLC), as well as appropriate morphology with minimum dead volume. So, MOFs with poor stability in water, methanol or acetonitrile are not suitable materials for RP-HPLC application. As most MOFs are stable in nonpolar organic solvents, MOFs are appropriate materials for NP-HPLC. However, direct packing of conventional MOF powders into HPLC columns in most previous works was not a good idea due to insufficient mechanical strength, large back pressure and poor column efficiency resulting from an irregular morphology. Immobilizing MOF nanoparticles on the surface of monodisperse silica microspheres or into monolithic polymer materials is the best way to overcome the above main obstacles for the utilization of MOFs as stationary phases for HPLC separation. Development of facile methods for bottom-up in situ growth of MOF nanoparticles chemically and uniformly bonded onto the monolithic matrixes is especially significant for future practical application of MOFs in HPLC. Good chemical stability in the GC mobile phase (He or ${\bf N}_{2}$ ) and excellent thermo-stability are the prerequisites for the materials as stationary phases for GC. In these aspects, most MOFs are excellent candidate materials for GC application as they are usually stable in the GC mobile phase from 250 to $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ In most previous works, MOF-based capillary columns are usually fabricated via a dynamic coating method, i.e. by slowly flushing slurries of MOFs through fused silica capillaries. Such a physical coating procedure easily leads to non-uniform and loose attachment of MOF particles on the inner surface of the capillary column with large intraparticle space. However, for practical use in GC, MOF nanoparticles should be firmly and uniformly attached on the inner surface of the capillary column with minimum intra-particle space in a thickness-controlled manner. Development of a practical method for fabricating such MOF-based capillary columns is challenging and urgent.
Under the spotlight, MOFs can provide various active sites or mechanisms (such as open metal sites, size exclusion, steric effect, Louis acid–base interactions, $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions) to interact with analytes for chromatographic separation. However, most previous explanations on the MOF-based chromatographic separation are speculative. An accurate understanding of the excellent performance of MOFs for chromatographic separation is urgently needed not only for selecting right available MOFs in further practical application, but also for further rational design of new MOFs for a particular separation.
Further research into biological based application of these materials is warranted; areas such as drug delivery, enzyme immobilization and the analysis/separation of biological samples are examples of fields that could really benefit.254,255 Equally important and interesting will be the combination of emerging porous materials with other functional materials, such as nanomaterials, magnetic and luminescent materials. This is a strategy that will enhance the performance of both materials and will further expand the applications of emerging porous materials.
A range of catalytic reactions under continuous flow have now been investigated using these porous materials directly as catalysts or as supports to attach active transition metal catalysts or nanoparticles. The area is still relatively underexplored and requires further research. Further comparisons are needed with conventional support materials such as silica or alumina in order to critically assess the potential advantages in terms of ease of functionalisation, catalytic activity, leaching and recyclability. The long-term stability of catalytic packing or coatings using these materials still remains a challenge. Porous materials coating may, however, provide greater functionality compared to silica supports which in turn may lead to greater control in catalyst loading. Catalysts with improved activity and resistance to leaching for flow synthesis, and a greater understanding of material structure, stability and reactivity of the catalytic scaffolds are needed. Such bespoke catalytic porous materials may find applications in multistep continuous flow processes where it is essential to ensure that the product is effectively separated from the catalyst prior to the next step of the reaction. One area of chemistry that may greatly benefit may be that of light driven reactions that require catalysts. Flow-based processes are particularly suited to conducting photochemical reactions since the penetration of light is more effective and uniform over the short distances typical of the diameters used in the channels of such reactors. Combining suitably catalytically active and optically transparent porous materials may facilitate such photochemical reactions. It is expected that microfluidic processes and their combination with porous materials will find applications in various emerging fields of research, for example: computer-controlled reactions, photocatalysis,256 environmental sciences and energy science, and will continue to be a rich area of research moving forward.
